225 152 1749 (         CP/M 3 recognizes two different types of commands: built-in commands and tran-) 1749 SB
225 211 1761 (        sient utitity commands. Built-in commands execute programs that reside in memory) 1761 SB
225 270 1801 (        as a part of the CP/M 3 operating system.  Built-in commands can be executed imme-) 1801 SB
225 330 1757 (        diately.  Transient utility commands are stored on disk as program files.  They must) 1757 SB
225 389 1740 (        be loaded from disk to perform their task.  You can recognize transient utility pro-) 1740 SB
225 448 1706 (        gram files when a directory is displayed on the screen because their filenames are) 1706 SB
225 507 1802 (        followed by COM.  Section 4 presents lists of the CP/M 3 built-in and transient utility) 1802 SB
225 566 329 (        commands.) 329 SB
225 685 1748 (          For transient utilities, CP/M 3 checks only the command keyword.  If you include) 1748 SB
225 744 1706 (        a command tail, CP/M 3 passes it to the utility without checking it because many) 1706 SB
225 803 1717 (        utilities require unique command tails.  A command tail cannot contain more than) 1717 SB
225 862 1778 (        128 characters.  Of course, CP/M 3 cannot read either the command keyword or the) 1778 SB
225 921 1057 (        command tail until you press the RETURN key.) 1057 SB
225 1040 1716 (          Let's use one command to demonstrate how CP/M 3 reads command lines.  The) 1716 SB
225 1099 1674 (        DIR command, which is -n abbreviation for directory, tells CP/M 3 to display a) 1674 SB
225 1158 1827 (        directory of disk files on your screeii.  Type the DIR keyword after the system prompt,) 1827 SB
225 1217 986 (        omit the command tail, and press RETURN.) 986 SB
225 1335 253 (        A>DIR) 253 SB
225 1454 1662 (        CP/M 3 responds to this command by writing the names of all the files that are) 1662 SB
225 1513 1752 (        stored on the disk in drive A. For example, if you have your CP/M 3 system disk in) 1752 SB
225 1572 1353 (        drive A, these filenames, among others, appear on your screen:) 1353 SB
225 1690 427 (        COPYSYS com) 427 SB
225 1750 392 (        PIP          com) 392 SB
225 1809 409 (        SET          com) 409 SB
1166 2933 67 (1-3) 67 SB
225 77 416 (CP/M 3 Users Guide) 416 SB
1704 77 471 (1.2 The Command Line) 471 SB
