251 894 475 (      2.1 What is a File?) 475 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 1013 1698 (          A CP/M 3 file is a collection of related information stored on a disk.  Every file) 1698 SB
225 1072 1678 (        must have a unique name because CP/M 3 uses that name to access that file.  A) 1678 SB
225 1131 1733 (        directory is also stored on each disk.  The directory contains a list of the filenames) 1733 SB
225 1190 1298 (        stored on that disk and the locations of each file on the disk.) 1298 SB
225 1309 1760 (          In general, there are two kinds of files: program \(command\) files and data files.  A) 1760 SB
225 1368 1735 (        program file contains an executable program, a series of instructions that the com-) 1735 SB
225 1427 1775 (        puter follows step-by-step.  A data file is usually a collection of information: a list of) 1775 SB
225 1486 1760 (        names and addresses, the inventory of a store, the accounting records of a business,) 1760 SB
225 1545 1761 (        the text of a document, or similar related information.  For example, your computer) 1761 SB
225 1605 1770 (        cannot execute names and addresses, but it can execute a program that prints names) 1770 SB
225 1664 735 (        and addresses on mailing labels.) 735 SB
225 1782 1727 (         A data file can also contain the source code for a program.  Generally, a program) 1727 SB
225 1841 1752 (        source file must be processed by an assembler or compiler before it becomes a pro-) 1752 SB
225 1900 1802 (        gram file.  In most cases, an executing program processes a data file.  However, there) 1802 SB
225 1960 1715 (        are times when an executing program processes a program file.  For example, the) 1715 SB
