225 153 26 (  ) 26 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
251 152 608 (   2.3 How Are Files Named?) 608 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 271 1724 (       CP/M 3 identifies every file by its unique file specification.  A file specification can) 1724 SB
225 331 1117 (     be simply a one- to eight-character filename, such as:) 1117 SB
225 449 250 (     MYFILE) 250 SB
225 567 1726 (       A file specification can have four parts: a drive specifier, a filename, a filetype, and) 1726 SB
225 626 302 (     a password.) 302 SB
225 745 1718 (       The drive specifier is a single letter \(A-P\) followed by a colon.  Each drive in your) 1718 SB
225 804 1648 (     system is assigned a letter.  When you include a drive specifier as part of the file) 1648 SB
225 863 1680 (     specification, you are telling CP/M 3 that the file is stored on the disk currently in) 1680 SB
225 922 793 (     that drive.  For example, if you enter) 793 SB
225 1041 297 (     B:MYFILE) 297 SB
225 1159 981 (     CP/M 3 looks in drive B for the file MYFILE.) 981 SB
225 1277 1711 (       The filename can be from one to eight characters.  When you make up a filename,) 1711 SB
225 1336 1705 (     try to let the name tell you something about what the file contains.  For example, if) 1705 SB
225 1396 1592 (     you have a list of customer names for your business, you could name the file:) 1592 SB
225 1514 337 (     CUSTOMER) 337 SB
225 1632 1222 (     so that the name gives you some idea of what is in the file.) 1222 SB
225 1751 1591 (       As you begin to use your computer with CP/M 3, you will find that files fall) 1591 SB
225 1810 1721 (     naturally into categories.  To help you identify files belonging to the same category,) 1721 SB
225 1869 1668 (     CP/M 3 allows you to add an optional one- to three-character extension, called a) 1668 SB
225 1928 1782 (     filetype, to the filename.  When you add a filetype to the filename, separate the filetype) 1782 SB
225 1987 1684 (     from the filename with a period.  Try to use three letters that tell something about) 1684 SB
225 2046 1731 (     the file's category.  For example, you could add the following filetype to the file that) 1731 SB
225 2106 736 (     contains a list of customer names:) 736 SB
225 2224 465 (     CUSTOMER.NAM) 465 SB
225 2342 1662 (     When CP/M 3 displays file specifications in response to a DIR command, it adds) 1662 SB
225 2401 1689 (     blanks to short filenames so that you can compare filetypes quickly.  The program) 1689 SB
225 2461 1655 (     files that CP/M 3 loads into memory from a disk have different filenames, but all) 1655 SB
225 2520 529 (     have the filetype COM.) 529 SB
1166 2934 67 (2-2) 67 SB
225 77 1564 (2.3 How Are Files Named?                                              CP/M 3 User's Guide) 1564 SB
