225 152 26 (  ) 26 SB
251 152 1727 (   CP/M  3 looks for the file MYFILE.LIB in the directory of the disk in drive B. When) 1727 SB
225 211 1619 (     you give a command to CP/M 3, note which disk is in the default drive.  Many) 1619 SB
225 270 1693 (     application programs require that the data files they access be stored in the default) 1693 SB
225 330 178 (     drive.) 178 SB
225 448 1753 (        You can also precede a program filename with a drive specifier, even if you use the) 1753 SB
225 507 1694 (     program filename as a command keyword.  For example, if you type the following) 1694 SB
225 566 258 (     command) 258 SB
225 685 300 (     A>B : P I P) 300 SB
225 803 1747 (     CP/M 3 looks in the directory of the disk in drive B for the file PIP.COM. If CP/M 3) 1747 SB
225 862 1310 (     finds PIP on drive B, it loads PIP into memory and executes it.) 1310 SB
225 980 1734 (        If you need to access many files on the same drive, you might find it convenient to) 1734 SB
225 1040 1747 (     change the default drive so that you do not need to repeatedly enter a drive specifier.) 1747 SB
225 1099 1718 (     To change the default drive, enter the drive specifier next to the system prompt and) 1718 SB
225 1158 1755 (     press RETURN.  In response, CP/M 3 changes the system prompt to display the new) 1755 SB
225 1217 328 (     default drive:) 328 SB
225 1335 175 (     A>B:) 175 SB
225 1395 126 (     B>) 126 SB
225 1513 1682 (        Unlike the filename and filetype which are stored in the disk directory, the drive) 1682 SB
225 1572 1784 (     specifier for a file changes as you move the disk from one drive to another.  Therefore,) 1784 SB
225 1631 1606 (     a file has a different file specification when you move a disk from one drive to) 1606 SB
225 1690 1724 (     another.  Section 4 presents more information on how CP/M 3 locates program and) 1724 SB
225 1750 255 (     data files.) 255 SB
225 1869 52 (    ) 52 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
277 1868 958 ( 2.5 Do You Have the Correct User Number?) 958 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 1987 1619 (        CP/M 3 further identifies all files by assigning each one a user number which) 1619 SB
225 2046 1612 (     ranges from 0 to 15.  CP/M 3 assigns the user number to a file when the file is) 1612 SB
225 2106 1758 (     created.  User numbers allow you to separate your files into sixteen file groups.  User) 1758 SB
225 2165 1367 (     numbers are particularly useful for organizing files on a hard disk.) 1367 SB
225 2283 1683 (        When you use a CP/M 3 utility to create a file, the file is assigned to the current) 1683 SB
225 2342 1702 (     user number, unless you use PIP to copy the file to another user number.  You can) 1702 SB
225 2401 1414 (     determine the current user number by looking at the system prompt.) 1414 SB
1166 2934 67 (2-4) 67 SB
225 77 1931 (2.4 Do You have the Correct Drive?                                                            CP/M 3 User's Guide) 1931 SB
