225 389 1771 (      The user number always precedes the drive identifier.  User 0, however, is the default) 1771 SB
225 448 1021 (      user number and is not displayed in the prompt.) 1021 SB
225 566 1645 (         You can use the built-in command USER to change the current user number.) 1645 SB
225 685 320 (      A >USER 3) 320 SB
225 744 166 (      3A>) 166 SB
225 862 1641 (         You can change both the user number and the drive by entering the new user) 1641 SB
225 921 1226 (      number and drive specifier together at the system prompt:) 1226 SB
225 1040 212 (      A >3B) 212 SB
225 1099 190 (      3 B >) 190 SB
225 1217 1766 (         Most commands can access only those files that have the current user number.  For) 1766 SB
225 1276 1710 (      example, if the current user number is 7, a DIR command with no options displays) 1710 SB
225 1335 1678 (      only the files that were created under user number 7. However, if a file resides in) 1678 SB
225 1395 1701 (      user 0 and is marked with a special file attribute, the file can be accessed from any) 1701 SB
225 1454 1127 (      user number. \(Section 2.7.1 discusses file attributes.\)) 1127 SB
225 1572 771 (      2.6 Accessing More Than One File) 771 SB
225 1690 1707 (         Certain CP/M 3 built-in and transient utilities can select and process several files) 1707 SB
225 1750 1767 (      when special wildcard characters are included in the filename or filetype.  A file spec-) 1767 SB
225 1809 1721 (      ification containing wildcards is called an ambiguous filespec and can refer to more) 1721 SB
225 1868 1735 (      than one file because it gives CP/M 3 a pattern to match.  CP/M 3 searches the disk) 1735 SB
225 1927 1589 (      directory and selects any file whose filename or filetype matches the pattern.) 1589 SB
