316 448 632 (2.7 How to Protect Your Files) 632 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 567 1627 (         Under CP/M 3 you can organize your files into groups to protect them from) 1627 SB
225 626 1772 (       accidental change and from unauthorized access.  You can specify how your files are) 1772 SB
225 686 1680 (       displayed in response to a DIR command, and monitor when your files were last) 1680 SB
225 745 1756 (       accessed or modified.  CP/M 3 supports these features by assigning the following to) 1756 SB
225 804 185 (       files:) 185 SB
225 922 472 (            o  user numbers) 472 SB
225 981 394 (            o  attributes) 394 SB
225 1041 625 (            o  time and date stamps) 625 SB
225 1100 873 (            o  passwords \(banked CP/M 3 only\)) 873 SB
225 1218 1433 (       All of this information for each file is recorded in the disk directory.) 1433 SB
225 1338 52 (    ) 52 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
277 1337 464 (   2.7.1 File Attributes) 464 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 1456 1745 (         File attributes control how a file can be accessed.  When you create a file, CP/M 3) 1745 SB
225 1515 1598 (       gives it two attributes. You can change the attributes with a SET command.) 1598 SB
225 1633 1666 (         The first attribute can be set to either DIR \(Directory\) or SYS \(System\).  This) 1666 SB
225 1693 1662 (       attribute controls whether CP/M 3 displays the file's name in response to a DIR) 1662 SB
225 1752 1770 (       command or DIRSYS command.  When you create a file, CP/M 3 automatically sets) 1770 SB
225 1811 1641 (       this attribute to DIR.  You can display the name of a file marked with the DIR) 1641 SB
225 1870 1743 (       attribute with a DIR command.  If you give a file the SYS attribute, you must use a) 1743 SB
225 1929 1793 (       DIRSYS command to display the filename.  Simple DIR and DIRSYS commands dis-) 1793 SB
225 1988 1327 (       play only the filenames created under the current user number.) 1327 SB
225 2107 1742 (         A file with the SYS attribute has a special advantage when it is created under user) 1742 SB
225 2166 1669 (       0. When you give a file with user number 0 the SYS attribute, you can read and) 1669 SB
225 2225 1743 (       execute that file from any user number.  This feature gives you a convenient way to) 1743 SB
225 2284 1744 (       make your commonly used programs available under any user number.  Note, how-) 1744 SB
225 2343 1811 (       ever, that a user 0 SYS file does not appear in response to a DIRSYS command unless) 1811 SB
225 2403 660 (       0 is the current user number.) 660 SB
1166 2935 67 (2-7) 67 SB
225 77 1921 (CP/M 3 User's Guide                                                                        2.6 Accessing Multiple Files) 1921 SB
EJ RS
