SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
0 0 0 fC
225 152 1659 (        The second file attribute can be set to either R/W \(Read-Write\) or R/O \(Read-) 1659 SB
225 211 1750 (     Only\).  If a file is marked R/0, any attempt to write data to that file produces a Read-) 1750 SB
225 270 1711 (     Only error message.  Therefore, you can use the R/O attribute to protect important) 1711 SB
225 330 1709 (     files.  A file with the R/W attribute can be read or written to, or erased at any time,) 1709 SB
225 389 939 (     unless the disk is physically write-protected.) 939 SB
225 508 52 (    ) 52 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
277 507 663 ( 2.7.2 Date and Time Stamping) 663 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 626 1719 (        If you use date and time stamps, you can quickly locate the most recent copy of a) 1719 SB
225 686 1678 (     file, and check when it was last updated or changed.  You can choose to have the) 1678 SB
225 745 1746 (     system tell you either when you created the file, or when you last read from or wrote) 1746 SB
225 804 1678 (     to the file.  You use the SET command to enable date and time stamping, and the) 1678 SB
225 863 1583 (     DIR command with the DATE option to display a file's time and date stamp.) 1583 SB
225 981 1759 (        A SET command enables the option you want to monitor.  You can use the follow-) 1759 SB
225 1041 1646 (     ing commands to enable time and date stamping on a disk, but you must choose) 1646 SB
225 1100 1767 (     between ACCESS and CREATE.  If you choose ACCESS, the stamp records the last) 1767 SB
225 1159 1738 (     time the file was accessed.  If you choose CREATE, the stamp records when the file) 1738 SB
225 1218 313 (     was created.) 313 SB
225 1336 553 (     A>SET [ACCESS=ON]) 553 SB
225 1396 559 (     A>SET [CREATE=ON]) 559 SB
225 1455 562 (     A>SET [UPDATE=ON]) 562 SB
225 1573 1718 (        Files created on or copied to a disk that has time and date stamping are automati-) 1718 SB
225 1632 1683 (     cally stamped.  The DATE command allows you to display and reset the time and) 1683 SB
225 1691 1739 (     date that CP/M 3 is using.  For a complete discussion of time and date stamping, see) 1739 SB
225 1751 1379 (     the descriptions of the SET and INITDIR commands in Section 5.) 1379 SB
225 1870 52 (    ) 52 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
277 1869 857 ( 2.7.3 Passwords \(Banked CP/M 3 Only\)) 857 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 1988 1754 (        Passwords allow you to protect your files from access by other users.  You can use) 1754 SB
225 2048 1304 (     passwords to limit access to certain files for security purposes.) 1304 SB
225 2166 1643 (        The SET utility allows you to enable password protection on a drive, assign a) 1643 SB
225 2225 1724 (     password to SET itself \(so that unauthorized users cannot disable password protec-) 1724 SB
225 2284 1757 (     tion on a drive\), and assign passwords to specific files that have already been created.) 1757 SB
