0 0 0 fC
225 152 1603 (          If you do not include a drive specifier with the filename in a command tail,) 1603 SB
225 211 1805 (         CP/M 3 searches the directory of the current user number on the default drive.  If the) 1805 SB
225 270 1764 (         file is not there, CP/M 3 looks for the file with the SYS attribute in the directory of) 1764 SB
225 330 1661 (         user 0 on the default drive.  If CP/M 3 finds the file under user 0, it allows the) 1661 SB
225 389 1783 (         program Read-Only access to the file.  For example, if you enter the following com-) 1783 SB
225 448 321 (         mand line,) 321 SB
225 566 640 (         3A>TYPE MYFILE.TXT) 640 SB
225 685 1610 (         CP/M 3 first searches the directory for user 3 on drive A. If it does not find) 1610 SB
225 744 1817 (         MYFILE.TXT there, it searches the directory of user 0 on drive A for MYFILE.TXT) 1817 SB
225 803 1766 (         marked with the SYS attribute.  If the file is not in either directory, CP/M 3 returns) 1766 SB
225 862 1090 (         control to TYPE, which then displays No F i 1 e.) 1090 SB
225 980 1773 (          Some CP/M 3 utilities, such as PIP and DIR, restrict their file search to the current) 1773 SB
225 1040 1863 (         user number.  Because CP/M 3 does not allow Read-Write access to SYS files,-ERASE) 1863 SB
225 1099 1451 (         and RENAME also restrict their search to the current user number.) 1451 SB
225 1217 1745 (          The search procedure is basically the same if you do include a drive specifier with) 1745 SB
225 1276 1758 (         the filename.  CP/M 3 first looks in the directory of the current user number on the) 1758 SB
225 1335 1751 (         specified drive.  Then, if it does not find the file, it looks in the directory for user 0) 1751 SB
225 1395 1806 (         on the specified drive for the file with the SYS attribute.  If CP/M 3 does not find the) 1806 SB
225 1454 1350 (         data file after these two searches, it displays an error message.) 1350 SB
225 1573 104 (        ) 104 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
329 1572 606 ( 4.4.2 Finding Program Files) 606 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 1691 1668 (           The search procedure for a program file can be very different from a data file) 1668 SB
225 1751 1809 (         search.  This is because you can use the SETDEF command described in Section 5 to) 1809 SB
225 1810 1697 (         define the search procedure you want CP/M 3 to follow when it is looking for a) 1697 SB
225 1869 1841 (         program file.  With SETDEF you can ask CP/M 3 to make as many as sixteen searches) 1841 SB
225 1928 1763 (         when you do not include a drive specifier before the command keyword, but that is) 1763 SB
225 1987 1713 (         a rare case!  We will begin by describing how CP/M 3 searches for program files) 1713 SB
225 2046 1177 (         when you have not yet entered a SETDEF command.) 1177 SB
225 2165 1785 (           If a command keyword identifies a transient utility, CP/M 3 looks for that program) 1785 SB
225 2224 1722 (         file on the default or specified drive.  It looks under the current user number, and) 1722 SB
225 2283 1749 (         then under user 0 for the same file marked with the SYS attribute.  At any point in) 1749 SB
225 2342 1805 (         the search process, CP/M 3 stops the search if it finds the program file.  CP/M 3 then) 1805 SB
225 2401 1692 (         loads the program into memory and executes it.  When the program terminates,) 1692 SB
225 2461 1779 (         CP/M 3 displays the system prompt and waits for your next command.  However, if) 1779 SB
225 2520 1753 (         CP/M 3 does not find the command file, it repeats the command line followed by a) 1753 SB
225 2579 1105 (         question mark, and waits for your next command.) 1105 SB
1166 2934 67 (4-5) 67 SB
225 77 1939 (CP/M 3 User's Guide                                                              4.4 How CP/M 3 Searches for Files) 1939 SB
