225 152 1607 (       If you include a drive specifier before the command keyword, you are telling) 1607 SB
225 211 1685 (    CP/M 3 precisely where to look for the program file.  Therefore, CP/M 3 searches) 1685 SB
225 270 1726 (    only two locations: the directory for the current user on the specified drive, and then) 1726 SB
225 330 1707 (    for user 0 on the specified drive, before it repeats the command line with a question) 1707 SB
225 389 692 (    mark.  For example, if you enter) 692 SB
225 507 524 (    4C>A:SH0W [SPACE]) 524 SB
225 625 1546 (    CP/M 3 looks on drive Al, user 4 and then user 0 for the file SHOW.COM.) 1546 SB
225 744 1610 (       If you do not include a drive specifier before the command keyword,CP/M 3) 1610 SB
225 803 1599 (    searches directories in a sequence called a drive chain.  When you first receive) 1599 SB
225 862 1704 (    CP/M 3, there is only one drive in your chain, the default drive.  Unless you change) 1704 SB
225 921 1673 (    the chain with a SETDEF command, CP/M 3 looks in two places for the program) 1673 SB
225 980 652 (    file.  For example, if you enter) 652 SB
225 1099 484 (    7E>SHOW [SPACE]) 484 SB
225 1217 1373 (    CP/M 3 searches the following locations for the file SHOW.COM:) 1373 SB
225 1335 485 (         1. drive E,   user 7) 485 SB
225 1395 485 (         2. drive E,   user 0) 485 SB
225 1513 1617 (    Remember that a SHOW.COM file under user 0 must be marked with the SYS) 1617 SB
225 1572 1693 (    attribute or else  CP/M 3 cannot find it. Use a SET command to give program files) 1693 SB
225 1631 1719 (    under user 0 to    the SYS attribute because they can then be accessed automatically) 1719 SB
225 1690 1746 (    from all other user areas.  You do not have to duplicate frequently used program files) 1746 SB
225 1750 625 (    in all user areas on all drives.) 625 SB
225 1868 1673 (       When you use a SETDEF command to define your own drive chain, include the) 1673 SB
225 1927 1707 (    default drive, and the drive that contains your most frequently used utilities.  For an) 1707 SB
225 1986 1685 (    example, assume you defined your drive chain as * \(the default drive\) and drive A.) 1685 SB
225 2045 863 (    When you enter the following command:) 863 SB
225 2164 478 (    2D>SH0W [SPACE]) 478 SB
1166 2933 67 (4-6) 67 SB
225 77 1939 (CP/M 3 User's Guide                                                              4.4 How CP/M 3 Searches for Files) 1939 SB
