225 152 1723 (        If you find you need to execute the same command sequence frequently, store the) 1723 SB
225 211 1688 (     sequence in a disk file.  To create this file, use ED or another character file editor.) 1688 SB
225 270 1732 (     The file must have a filetype of SUB.  Each command in the file must start on a new) 1732 SB
225 330 1296 (     line.  For example, an UPDATE.SUB file might look like this:) 1296 SB
225 448 364 (     DIR A:*.COM) 364 SB
225 507 375 (     ERA B:*.COM) 375 SB
225 566 426 (     PIP B:=A:*.COM) 426 SB
225 685 1075 (     To execute this list,, enter the following command:) 1075 SB
225 803 510 (     A>SUBMT UPDATE) 510 SB
225 921 1785 (     The SUBMIT utility passes each command to CP/M 3 for sequential execution.  While) 1785 SB
225 980 1715 (     SUBMIT executes, the commands are usually echoed at the console, as well as any) 1715 SB
225 1040 1662 (     program's screen display, such as the directory or PIP's "COPYING..." message.) 1662 SB
225 1099 1679 (     When one command completes, the system prompt reappears either with the next) 1679 SB
225 1158 1683 (     command in the SUB file, or, when the SUB file is exhausted, by itself to wait for) 1683 SB
225 1217 852 (     your next command from the keyboard.) 852 SB
225 1335 1721 (        If PROFILE exists, PROFILE.SUB is a special submit file that CP/M 3 automati-) 1721 SB
225 1395 1759 (     cally executes at each cold start.  This feature is especially convenient if you regularly) 1759 SB
225 1454 1771 (     execute a standard set of commands, such as SETDEF and DATE SET, before begin-) 1771 SB
225 1513 1755 (     ning a work session.  A PROFILE.SUB might already exist on your distribution disk.) 1755 SB
225 1572 1134 (     If not, you can create one using ED or another editor.) 1134 SB
225 1690 1705 (        The description of the SUBMIT utility in Section 5 gives more details on how to) 1705 SB
225 1750 1754 (     create a SUB file and use SUBMIT parameters to pass options to the programs to be) 1754 SB
225 1809 255 (     executed.) 255 SB
225 1927 1665 (        You can also use CTRL-C to reset the disk system.  This is sometimes called a) 1665 SB
225 1986 1614 (     warm start.  When you press CTRL-C and the cursor Is at the system prompt,) 1614 SB
225 2045 1716 (     CP/M 3 logs out all the active drives, then logs in the default drive. ne active drives) 1716 SB
225 2105 1800 (     are any drives you have accessed since the last cold or warm start.  A SHOW [SPACE]) 1800 SB
225 2164 1762 (     command displays the remaining space on all active drives.  In the following example,) 1762 SB
225 2223 1804 (     SHOW [SPACE] indicates that three drives are active.  However, if you press CTRL-C) 1804 SB
225 2282 1778 (     immediately after this display and then enter another SHOW [SPACE] command, only) 1778 SB
225 2341 985 (     the space for the default drive, A, is displayed.) 985 SB
