225 271 1758 (        You can use the two keystroke command CTRL-C to terminate program execution) 1758 SB
225 331 1719 (      or reset the disk system.  To enter a CTRL-C command, hold down the CTRL key) 1719 SB
225 390 323 (      and press C.) 323 SB
225 508 1556 (        Not all application programs that run under CP/M can be terminated by a) 1556 SB
225 567 1643 (      CTRL-C, However, most of the transient utilities supplied with CP/M 3 can be) 1643 SB
225 626 1729 (      terminated immediately by a CTRL-C keystroke.  If you try to terminate a program) 1729 SB
225 686 1746 (      while it is sending a display to the screen, you might need to press a CTRL-S to halt) 1746 SB
225 745 806 (      the display before entering CTRL-C.) 806 SB
225 863 1665 (        You can also use CTRL-C to reset the disk system.  This is sometimes called a) 1665 SB
225 922 1622 (      warm start.  When you press CTRL-C and the cursor is at the system prompt,) 1622 SB
225 981 1773 (      CP/M 3 logs out all the active drives, then logs in the default drive.  The active drives) 1773 SB
225 1041 1813 (      are any drives you have accessed since the last cold or warm start.  A SHOW [SPACE]) 1813 SB
225 1100 1720 (      command displays the remaining space on all active drives.  In the following exam-) 1720 SB
225 1159 1712 (      ple, SHOW [SPACE] indicates that three drives are active.  However, if you press) 1712 SB
225 1218 1664 (      CTRL-C immediately after this display and then enter another SHOW [SPACE]) 1664 SB
225 1277 1315 (      command, only the space for the default drive, A, is displayed.) 1315 SB
