225 152 1690 (         Let's look at some examples of syntax notation.  The CP/M 3 DIR \(DIRectory\)) 1690 SB
225 211 1758 (        command displays the names of files cataloged in the disk directory and, optionally,) 1758 SB
225 270 926 (        displays other information about the files.) 926 SB
225 389 1689 (          The syntax of the DIR command with options shows how to use the command) 1689 SB
225 448 505 (        line syntax notation:) 505 SB
225 566 995 (            Syntax:     DIR{fd:{|}filespec}{[options]}) 995 SB
225 625 1174 (                                        |            |               |                     ) 1174 SB
225 685 980 (                             optional     optional    optional) 980 SB
225 803 1812 (        This tells you that the command tail following the command keyword DIR is optional.) 1812 SB
225 862 1702 (        DIR alone is a valid command, but you can include a file specification, or a drive) 1702 SB
225 921 1395 (        specification, or 'ust the options in the command line.  Therefore,) 1395 SB
225 1040 242 (            DIR) 242 SB
225 1099 404 (            DIR filespec) 404 SB
225 1158 294 (            DIR d:) 294 SB
225 1217 358 (            DIR [RO]) 358 SB
225 1335 1751 (        are valid commands. Furthermore, the drive or file specification can be followed by) 1751 SB
225 1395 1639 (        another optional value selected from one of the following list of DIR options:) 1639 SB
225 1513 225 (            RO) 225 SB
225 1572 235 (            RW) 235 SB
225 1631 242 (            DIR) 242 SB
225 1690 248 (            SYS) 248 SB
225 1809 313 (        Therefore,) 313 SB
225 1927 559 (            DIR d:filespec [RO]) 559 SB
225 2045 497 (        is a valid command.) 497 SB
225 2164 1685 (          Recall that in Section 2 you learned about wildcards in filenames and filetypes.) 1685 SB
225 2223 1320 (        The DIR command accepts wildcards in the file specification,) 1320 SB
1166 2933 67 (5-7) 67 SB
