225 744 1722 (        The CP/M 3 command PIP \(Peripheral Interchange Program\) is the file copy pro-) 1722 SB
225 803 1661 (      gram.  PIP can copy information from the disk to the screen or printer.  PIP can) 1661 SB
225 862 1796 (      combine two or more files into one longer file.  PIP can also rename files after copying) 1796 SB
225 921 1717 (      them.  Look at one of the formats of the PIP command line for another example of) 1717 SB
225 980 1703 (      how to use command line notation.  PIP also copies files from one disk to another) 1703 SB
225 1040 172 (      disk.) 172 SB
225 1158 1246 (           Syntax:    PIP dest-filespec = src-filespecf{,filespec ... }) 1246 SB
225 1276 1748 (        In the preceding example, dest-filespec is further defined as a destination file speci-) 1748 SB
225 1335 1747 (      fication or peripheral device \(printer, for example\) that receives data.  Similarly, src-) 1747 SB
225 1395 1773 (      filespec is a source file specification or peripheral device \(keyboard, for example\) that) 1773 SB
225 1454 1765 (      transmits data.  PIP accepts wildcards in the filename and filetype. \(See the PIP com-) 1765 SB
225 1513 1772 (      mand for details regarding other capabilities of PIP.\) There are, of course, many valid) 1772 SB
225 1572 1414 (      command lines that come from this syntax.  Some examples follow.) 1414 SB
225 1690 886 (      PIP    NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT) 886 SB
225 1750 659 (      PIP    B:=A:THISFILE.DAT) 659 SB
225 1809 733 (      PIP    B:X.BAS=Y.BAS, Z.BAS) 733 SB
225 1868 856 (      PIP    X.BAS=A.BAS, B.BAS, C.BAS) 856 SB
225 1927 468 (      PIP    B:=A:*.BAK) 468 SB
225 1986 390 (      PIP    B:=A:*.*) 390 SB
