580 391 1365 (   PIP is a transient utility that copies one or more files from one disk) 1365 SB
225 450 1662 (                     and/or user number to another.  PIP can rename a file after copying it.) 1662 SB
225 509 1639 (                     PIP can combine two or more files into one file.  PIP can also copy a) 1639 SB
225 568 1648 (                     character file from disk to the printer or other auxiliary logical output) 1648 SB
225 628 1616 (                     device.  PIP can create a file on disk from input from the console or) 1616 SB
225 687 1630 (                     other logical input device.  PIP can transfer data from a logical input) 1630 SB
225 746 1679 (                     device to a logical output device, thus the name Peripheral Interchange) 1679 SB
225 805 458 (                     Program.) 458 SB
225 923 1605 (                     PIP copies file attributes with the file.  This includes Read-Write or) 1605 SB
225 983 1664 (                     Read-Only and SYS or DIR file attributes and the user-definable attri-) 1664 SB
225 1042 1692 (                     butes Fl through F4.  If a file is password-protected, you must enter the) 1692 SB
225 1101 1682 (                     password in the command line following the filename and/or filetype to) 1682 SB
225 1160 1703 (                     which it belongs.  If the password fails, the file is skipped and the failure) 1703 SB
225 1219 398 (                     noted.) 398 SB
225 1338 1643 (                     When you specify a destination file with a password, PIP assigns that) 1643 SB
225 1397 1619 (                     password to the destination file and automatically sets the password) 1619 SB
225 1456 1696 (                     protection mode to READ.  When you specify a destination file with no) 1696 SB
225 1515 1701 (                     password, PIP does not assign a password to the destination file.  When) 1701 SB
225 1574 1636 (                              'fy only a destination drive, PIP assigns the same password and) 1636 SB
225 1633 460 (                     you speci) 460 SB
225 1693 1597 (                     password protection mode to the destination file as specified in the) 1597 SB
225 1752 1645 (                     source file.  When you specify a destination file with a password, PIP) 1645 SB
225 1811 1706 (                     automatically sets the password protection mode to READ.  This means) 1706 SB
225 1870 1608 (                     that you need a password to read the file. \(See the SET command.\)) 1608 SB
