567 153 1418 (  The first form shows the simplest way to copy a file. PIP looks for the) 1418 SB
225 212 1613 (                   file named by src-filespec on the default or optionally specified drive.) 1613 SB
225 271 1578 (                   PIP copies the file to the drive specified by d: and gives it the name) 1578 SB
225 331 1622 (                   specified by src-filespec.  If you want, you can use the [Gn] option to) 1622 SB
225 390 1679 (                   place your destination file \(dest-filespec\) in the user number specified by) 1679 SB
225 449 1628 (                   n. The only option recognized for the destination file is [Gn].  Several) 1628 SB
225 508 1647 (                   options can be combined together for the source file specification \(src-) 1647 SB
225 567 1099 (                   filespec\).  See the Table 5-13, PIP options.) 1099 SB
225 686 1663 (                   The second form is a variation of the first.  PIP looks for the file named) 1663 SB
225 745 1628 (                   by dest-filespec on the drive specified by d:, copies it to the default or) 1628 SB
225 804 1708 (                   optionally specified drive, and gives it the name specified by dest-filespec.) 1708 SB
225 922 1599 (                   The third form shows how to rename the file after you copy it.  You) 1599 SB
225 981 1611 (                   can copy it to the same drive and user number, or to a different drive) 1611 SB
225 1041 1653 (                   and/or user number.  Rules for options are the same.  PIP looks for the) 1653 SB
225 1100 1631 (                   file specified by src-filespec, copies it to the location specified in dest-) 1631 SB
225 1159 1365 (                   filespec, and gives it the name indicated by dest-filespec.) 1365 SB
225 1277 1606 (                   Remember that PIP always goes to and gets from the current default) 1606 SB
225 1336 1505 (                   user number unless you specify otherwise with the [Gn] option.) 1505 SB
225 1455 1630 (                   Before you start PIP, be sure that you have enough free space in kilo-) 1630 SB
225 1514 1592 (                   bytes on your destination disk to hold the entire file or files that you) 1592 SB
225 1573 1623 (                   are copying.  Even if you are replacing an old copy on the destination) 1623 SB
225 1632 1601 (                   disk with a new copy, PIP still needs enough room for the new copy) 1601 SB
225 1691 1580 (                   before it deletes the old copy.  Use the DIR command to determine) 1580 SB
225 1751 1614 (                   filesize and the SHOW command to determine disk space.  If there is) 1614 SB
225 1810 1679 (                   not enough space, you can delete the old copy first by using the ERASE) 1679 SB
225 1869 453 (                   command.) 453 SB
225 1987 1657 (                   Data is first copied to a temporary file to ensure that the entire data file) 1657 SB
225 2046 1582 (                   can be constructed in the space available on the disk.  PIP gives the) 1582 SB
225 2106 1617 (                   temporary file the filename specified for the destination, with the file-) 1617 SB
225 2165 1694 (                   type $$$.  If the copy operation is successful, PIP changes the temporary) 1694 SB
225 2224 1320 (                   filetype $$$ to the filetype specified in the destination.) 1320 SB
225 2342 1579 (                   If the copy operation succeeds and a file with the same name as the) 1579 SB
225 2401 1643 (                   destination file already exists, the old file with the same name is erased) 1643 SB
225 2461 947 (                   before renaming the temporary file.) 947 SB
