225 1396 1684 (                      Both forms of this command cause PIP to read the file oldfile.dat from) 1684 SB
225 1455 1597 (                      drive A and put an exact copy of it onto drive B. This is called the) 1597 SB
225 1514 1703 (                      short form of PIP, because the source or destination names only a drive) 1703 SB
225 1573 1715 (                      and does not include a filename.  When using this form you cannot copy) 1715 SB
225 1632 1564 (                      a file from one drive and user number to the same drive and user) 1564 SB
225 1691 1656 (                      number.  You must put the destination file on a different drive or in a) 1656 SB
225 1751 1684 (                      different user number. \(See the section on PIP Options, and the USER) 1684 SB
225 1810 1694 (                      Command.\) The second short form produces exactly the same result as) 1694 SB
225 1869 1611 (                      the first one.  PIP looks for the file oldfile.dat on drive A, the drive) 1611 SB
225 1928 745 (                      specified as the source.) 745 SB
225 2046 1002 (                      A > PIP B:newfile.dat=A:oldfile.dat) 1002 SB
225 2165 1643 (                      This command copies the file oldfile.dat from drive A to drive B and) 1643 SB
225 2224 1620 (                      renames it to newfile.dat. The file remains as oldfile.dat on drive A.) 1620 SB
225 2283 1649 (                      This is the long form of the PIP command, because it names a file on) 1649 SB
225 2342 915 (                      both sides of the command line.) 915 SB
