225 152 1632 (                   A symbolic expression can be a name from a SYM file produced from) 1632 SB
225 211 1595 (                   your program by a CP/M Macro Assembler.  When you precede the) 1595 SB
225 270 1652 (                   symbolic expression with a period, SID returns its address in hexadeci-) 1652 SB
225 330 1614 (                   mal.  When you precede the symbolic expression with the at sign, @,) 1614 SB
225 389 1661 (                   SID returns the 16-bit value stored at that location and the next contig-) 1661 SB
225 448 1687 (                   uous location.  When you precede the symbolic expression with an equal) 1687 SB
225 507 1632 (                   sign, SID returns the 8-bit value stored at that location.  For two-byte) 1632 SB
225 566 1681 (                   expressions, this is the low byte because the 8080 microprocessor stores) 1681 SB
225 625 1014 (                   the low value of a two-byte word first.) 1014 SB
225 744 1646 (                   A symbolic expression can be a literal value in hex, decimal, or ASCII,) 1646 SB
225 803 889 (                   as indicated in the following list:) 889 SB
225 921 1546 (                   o   SID uses literal hex values as given, but truncates any digits in) 1546 SB
225 980 1626 (                       excess of four on the left.  The leftmost digit is the most significant) 1626 SB
225 1040 1360 (                       digit.  The rightmost digit is the least significant digit.) 1360 SB
225 1099 1534 (                   o   To indicate decimal values precede them with a pound sign, #) 1534 SB
225 1158 1616 (                       Decimal values that evaluate to more than four hex digits are eval-) 1616 SB
225 1217 1375 (                       uated as the modulo of hex value FFFF.  For example,) 1375 SB
225 1276 1149 (                       #65534 = FFFEH, while #65536 = 0001H.) 1149 SB
225 1335 1632 (                   o   SID translates literal ASCII character strings between apostrophes) 1632 SB
225 1395 1407 (                       to the hex value of the two rightmost ASCII characters.) 1407 SB
225 1513 1643 (                   You can combine symbolic expressions with the symbolic operators, +) 1643 SB
225 1572 1601 (                   or -, to produce another symbolic expression.  Symbolic expressions) 1601 SB
225 1631 1659 (                   combined in this way can be used to calculate the offset of an indirectly) 1659 SB
225 1690 1642 (                   addressed data item, for example a subscripted variable.  A special up-) 1642 SB
225 1750 1605 (                   arrow operator, , can reference the top-of-stack item.  A string of n ') 1605 SB
225 1809 1653 (                   operators can reference the nth stack item without changing stack con-) 1653 SB
225 1868 736 (                   tent or the stack pointer.) 736 SB
225 1986 1621 (                   Table 5-18 lists the SID commands with their corresponding parame-) 1621 SB
225 2045 1676 (                   ters and options.  The actual command letter is printed in boldface.  The) 1676 SB
225 2105 1647 (                   parameters are in lower-case and follow the command letter.  Optional) 1647 SB
225 2164 1628 (                   items are in braces.  Replace the arguments with the appropriate sym-) 1628 SB
225 2223 1687 (                   bolic expressions as listed.  Where two symbolic expressions are needed,) 1687 SB
225 2282 1559 (                   SID can calculate the second one from the first using the symbolic) 1559 SB
225 2341 868 (                   operators described previously.) 868 SB
1141 2933 117 (5-104) 117 SB
