225 389 1658 (    Explanation:  The USER command sets the current user number. When you start) 1658 SB
225 448 1641 (                  CP/M 3, 0 is the current user number.  You can use a USER command) 1641 SB
225 507 1495 (                  to change the current user number to another in the range 0-15.) 1495 SB
225 625 1588 (                  CP/M 3 identifies every file with a user number.  In general, you can) 1588 SB
225 685 1610 (                  access only files identified with the current user number.  However, if) 1610 SB
225 744 1660 (                  you mark a file in user 0 with the SYS attribute, the file can be accessed) 1660 SB
225 803 795 (                  from all other user numbers.) 795 SB
225 921 540 (    Examples:     A > USER) 540 SB
225 1040 1485 (                  The system command prompts for the user number, as follows:) 1485 SB
