329 598 485 (6.1 Introduction to ED) 485 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 717 1724 (          To do almost anything with a computer you need some way to enter data, a way) 1724 SB
225 776 1715 (        to give the computer the information you want it to process.  The programs most) 1715 SB
225 835 1771 (        commonly used for this task are called editors.  They transfer your keystrokes at the) 1771 SB
225 895 1704 (        keyboard to a disk file.  CP/M 3's editor is named ED.  Using ED, you can easily) 1704 SB
225 954 774 (        create and alter CP/M 3 text files.) 774 SB
225 1072 1555 (          The correct command format for invoking the CP/M 3 editor is given in) 1555 SB
225 1131 1782 (        Section 6.2, "Starting ED." After starting ED, you issue commands that transfer text) 1782 SB
225 1190 1695 (        from a disk file to memory for editing.  Section 6.3, "ED Operation," details this) 1695 SB
225 1250 1710 (        operation and describes the basic text transfer commands that allow you to easily) 1710 SB
225 1309 591 (        enter and exit the editor.) 591 SB
225 1427 349 (                       44) 349 SB
225 1486 1652 (          Section 6.4, Basic Editing Commands," details the commands that edit a file.) 1652 SB
225 1545 1770 (        Section 1,1, "Combining ED Commands," describes how to combine the basic com-) 1770 SB
225 1605 1696 (        mands to edit more efficiently.  Although you can edit any file with the basic ED) 1696 SB
225 1664 1712 (        commands, ED provides several more commands that perform more complicated) 1712 SB
225 1723 1583 (        editing functions, as described in Section 6.6, "Advanced ED Commands.") 1583 SB
225 1841 1785 (          During an editing session, ED can return two types of error messages.  Section 6.7,) 1785 SB
225 1900 1771 (        "ED Error Messages," lists these messages and provides examples that indicate how) 1771 SB
225 1960 1092 (        to recover from common editing error conditions.) 1092 SB
225 2079 91 (       ) 91 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c370 font
316 2078 346 ( 6.2 Starting ED) 346 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c363 font
225 2197 254 (        Syntax:) 254 SB
225 2316 931 (            ED input-filespec {d: I output-filespecl) 931 SB
225 2434 1747 (          To start ED, enter its name after the CP/M 3 prompt.  The command ED must be) 1747 SB
225 2493 1718 (        followed by a file specification, one that contains no wildcard characters, such as:) 1718 SB
225 2611 569 (        A > ED MYFILE.TEX) 569 SB
