225 152 1761 (        The file specification, MYFILE.TEX in the preceding example, specifies a file to be) 1761 SB
225 211 1756 (      edited or created.  The file specification can be preceded by a drive specification, but) 1756 SB
225 270 1691 (      a drive specification is unnecessary if the file to be edited is on your default drive.) 1691 SB
225 330 1761 (      Optionally, the file specification can be followed by a drive specification, as shown in) 1761 SB
225 389 531 (      the following example:) 531 SB
225 507 577 (      A>ED MYFILE.TEX B:) 577 SB
225 625 1690 (        In response to this command, ED opens the file to be edited, MYFILE.TEX, on) 1690 SB
225 685 1275 (      drive A, but sends all the edited material to a file on drive B.) 1275 SB
225 803 1699 (        Optionally, you can send the edited material to a file with a different filename, as) 1699 SB
225 862 580 (      in the following example:) 580 SB
225 980 887 (      A>ED MYFILE.TEX YOURFILE.TEX) 887 SB
225 1099 1731 (        The file with the different filename cannot already exist or ED prints the following) 1731 SB
225 1158 559 (      message and terminates.) 559 SB
225 1276 620 (      Output File Exists, Erase It) 620 SB
225 1395 1725 (        The ED prompt, *, appears at the screen when ED is ready to accept a command,) 1725 SB
225 1454 278 (      as follows) 278 SB
225 1572 517 (      A>ED MYFILE.TEX) 517 SB
375 1631 52 (: *) 52 SB
225 1750 1622 (        If no previous version of the file exists on the current disk, ED automatically) 1622 SB
225 1809 1143 (      creates a new file and displays the following message:) 1143 SB
225 1927 309 (      NEW FILE) 309 SB
375 1986 52 (: *) 52 SB
225 2105 1640 (      Note: before starting an editing session, use the SHOW command to check the) 1640 SB
225 2164 1728 (      amount of free space on your disk.  Make sure that the unused portion of your disk) 1728 SB
225 2223 1712 (      is at least as large as the file you are editing, or larger if you plan to add characters) 1712 SB
225 2282 1667 (      to the file.  When ED finds a disk or directory full, ED has only limited recovery) 1667 SB
225 2341 1507 (      mechanisms.  These are explained in Section 6.7, "ED Error Messages.") 1507 SB
