225 271 1764 (           With ED, you change portions of a file that pass through a memory buffer.  When) 1764 SB
225 331 1787 (         you start ED with one of the preceding commands, this memory buffer is empty.  At) 1787 SB
225 390 1750 (         your command, ED reads segments of the source file, for example MYFILE.TEX,) 1750 SB
225 449 1741 (         into the memory buffer for you to edit.  If the file is new, you must insert text into) 1741 SB
225 508 1803 (         the file before you can edit.  During the edit, ED writes the edited text onto a tempo-) 1803 SB
225 567 699 (         rary work file, MYFILE.$$$.) 699 SB
225 686 1723 (           When you end the edit, ED writes the memory buffer contents to the temporary) 1723 SB
225 745 1779 (         file, followed by any remaining text in the source file.  ED then changes the name of) 1779 SB
225 804 1823 (         the source file from MYFILE.TEX to MYFILE.BAK, so you can reclaim this original) 1823 SB
225 863 1706 (         material from the back-up file if necessary.  ED then renames the temporary file,) 1706 SB
225 922 1792 (         MYFILE.$$$, to MYFILE.TEX, the new edited file. The following figure illustrates) 1792 SB
225 981 1739 (         the relationship between the source file, the temporary work file, and the new file.) 1739 SB
225 1100 1747 (         Note: when you invoke ED with two filespecs, an input file and an output file, ED) 1747 SB
225 1159 1727 (         does not rename the input file to type BAK; therefore, the input file can be Read-) 1727 SB
225 1218 1635 (         Only or on a write-protected disk if the output file is written to another disk.) 1635 SB
1166 2934 67 (6-3) 67 SB
225 77 1897 (6.3 ED Operation                                                                                      CP/M 3 User's Guide) 1897 SB
