225 271 1720 (      The text transfer commands discussed previously allow you to easily enter and exit) 1720 SB
225 331 1425 (    the editor.  This section discusses the basic commands that edit a file.) 1425 SB
225 449 1736 (      ED treats a file as a long chain of characters grouped together in lines.  ED displays) 1736 SB
225 508 1700 (    and edits characters and lines in relation to an imaginary device called the character) 1700 SB
225 567 1715 (    pointer \(CP\).  During an edit session, you must mentally picture the CP's location in) 1715 SB
225 626 1494 (    the memory buffer and issue commands to move the CP and edit the file.) 1494 SB
225 745 1703 (      The following commands move the character pointer or display text in the vicinity) 1703 SB
225 804 1675 (    of the CP.  These ED commands consist of a numeric argument and a single com-) 1675 SB
225 863 1666 (    mand letter and must be followed by a carriage return.  The numeric argument, n,) 1666 SB
225 922 1673 (    determines the number of times ED executes a command; however, there are four) 1673 SB
225 981 1241 (    special cases to consider in regard to the numeric argument:) 1241 SB
225 1100 1489 (        o   If the numeric argument is omitted, ED assumes an argument of 1.) 1489 SB
225 1159 1683 (        o   Use a negative number if the command is to be executed backwards through) 1683 SB
225 1218 1212 (            the memory buffer.  The B command is an exception.) 1212 SB
225 1277 1681 (        o   If you enter a pound sign, #, in place of a number, ED uses the value 65,535) 1681 SB
225 1336 1681 (            as the argument.  A pound sign argument can be preceded by a minus sign to) 1681 SB
225 1396 1815 (            cause the command to execute backwards through the memory buffer, -#.               ) 1815 SB
225 1455 1598 (        o   ED accepts 0 as a numeric argument only in certain commands. In some) 1598 SB
225 1514 1663 (            cases, 0 causes the command to be executed approximately half the possible) 1663 SB
225 1573 1622 (            number of times, while in other cases it prevents the movement of the CP.) 1622 SB
1166 2934 67 (6-8) 67 SB
225 77 1924 (6.4 Basic Editing Commands                                                                       CP/M 3 User's Guide) 1924 SB
