225 270 1640 (         The inverse of the line number command specifies that a command should be) 1640 SB
225 330 1750 (       executed through a certain line number.  You can use this command with only three) 1750 SB
225 389 1748 (       ED commands: the K \(kill\) command, the L \(line\) command, and the T \(type\) com-) 1748 SB
225 448 1085 (       mand.  The :n command takes the following form:) 1085 SB
225 566 387 (            :ncommand) 387 SB
225 685 1706 (       where n is the line number through which the command is to be executed.  The :n) 1706 SB
225 744 1754 (       part of the command does not move the CP, but the command that follows it might.) 1754 SB
225 862 1727 (         You can combine n: with :n to specify a range of lines through which a command) 1727 SB
225 921 1730 (       should be executed.  For example, the command 2::4T types the second, third, and) 1730 SB
225 980 329 (       fourth lines:) 329 SB
225 1099 316 (       1 :  *2: :4T) 316 SB
225 1158 625 (       2: "I find ecstasy in living -) 625 SB
225 1217 610 (       3: the mere sense of living) 610 SB
225 1276 479 (       4:    is joy enough.") 479 SB
